  "note": "Idle ledgers change nothing: with 0 transactions neither the growth nor the cut branch runs, so txnsExpected stays where the last busy ledger left it until a slow-consensus ledger halves it. The cheap-transaction allowance therefore stays elevated after a flood, which helps legitimate users but also gives the next flood a higher starting allowance. The flood scenarios assume the attacker pays the escalated minimum at every position in every ledger (worst-case willingness to pay). Under that assumption the multiplier compounds ~2.4x per 100-tx ledger, and the required fee exceeds the entire 100-billion-PFT supply within a few ledgers. In practice nobody can pay that, so transactions queue instead of entering the ledger and the multiplier stops compounding: the queue, not the open ledger, absorbs a sustained flood. Fee levels are capped at the uint64 maximum, as the C++ mulDiv does."
 }
